---
import DocsLayout from '~/layouts/DocsLayout.astro'
---

<DocsLayout title="Migrating" description="Moving to Kotoshu from hunspell, aspell, cspell, or LanguageTool — what transfers, what needs a step, and what is not there yet.">
  <p class="docs-lead">Bringing your words with you is a command or two; bringing your workflow depends on the tool you are leaving.</p>

  <p class="eyebrow mb-3"><span class="jp" lang="ja">移行</span>Migrate</p>
  <h2 class="display text-2xl mt-12">From hunspell</h2>
  <p class="text-[15px] leading-7 text-[var(--color-text-2)] mt-4">
    Bring your own dictionaries. Register the .aff/.dic pair you already have and
    nothing is re-downloaded:
  </p>
  <pre class="code-block overflow-x-auto p-4 text-[13px] leading-7 mt-4"><code>kotoshu setup en --aff en_US.aff --dic en_US.dic

# or point at a directory — it expects LANG.aff and LANG.dic
kotoshu setup en --from /usr/share/hunspell/</code></pre>
  <p class="text-[15px] leading-7 text-[var(--color-text-2)] mt-4">
    Because the engine is a Ruby port of the Hunspell algorithm — with behavior
    tested against Hunspell's own reference fixtures — affix rules, flag
    conditions, and compound handling behave the way your dictionaries expect.
    The difference you will notice is operational, not linguistic: resources are
    explicit (setup once, cache-only after) and results come out as JSON or SARIF
    in addition to text.
  </p>

  <h2 class="display text-2xl mt-12">From aspell</h2>
  <p class="text-[15px] leading-7 text-[var(--color-text-2)] mt-4">
    Kotoshu does not read aspell's compiled .rws dictionaries; point it at
    Hunspell-format files or a plain word list instead
    (<code class="font-mono text-[13px] text-[var(--color-accent)]">dictionary_type :plain_text</code>
    or <code class="font-mono text-[13px] text-[var(--color-accent)]">:hunspell</code>). Aspell
    personal word lists (.pws) are not auto-imported yet — export yours as one
    word per line and import it once:
  </p>
  <pre class="code-block overflow-x-auto p-4 text-[13px] leading-7 mt-4"><code>kotoshu personal import my-words.txt</code></pre>
  <p class="text-[15px] leading-7 text-[var(--color-text-2)] mt-4">
    After that, <code class="font-mono text-[13px] text-[var(--color-accent)]">kotoshu personal add</code>
    and the CLI's interactive review keep the personal dictionary current by hand.
  </p>

  <h2 class="display text-2xl mt-12">From cspell</h2>
  <p class="text-[15px] leading-7 text-[var(--color-text-2)] mt-4">
    cspell's configuration maps cleanly onto Kotoshu's model, with one honest gap:
    cspell's committed-in-repo cspell.json ergonomics has no Kotoshu equivalent
    yet — configuration lives in XDG files and environment variables.
  </p>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto mt-6"><table class="w-full text-sm">
    <thead>
      <tr class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-[var(--color-text-3)]">
        <th class="px-3 py-2.5 text-left">cspell</th>
        <th class="px-3 py-2.5 text-left">Kotoshu</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr class="border-t border-[var(--color-border)]">
        <td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-[var(--color-text-2)]">dictionaries / language settings in cspell.json</td>
        <td class="px-3 py-2.5"><code class="font-mono text-[13px] text-[var(--color-accent)]">kotoshu setup LANG</code>, one per language</td>
      </tr>
      <tr class="border-t border-[var(--color-border)]">
        <td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-[var(--color-text-2)]">words lists, projectWords</td>
        <td class="px-3 py-2.5">personal dictionary — <code class="font-mono text-[13px] text-[var(--color-accent)]">kotoshu personal import words.txt</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr class="border-t border-[var(--color-border)]">
        <td class="px-3 py-2.5 text-[var(--color-text-2)]">reporters (json, junit)</td>
        <td class="px-3 py-2.5"><code class="font-mono text-[13px] text-[var(--color-accent)]">--format json</code> and <code class="font-mono text-[13px] text-[var(--color-accent)]">--format sarif</code>, plus stable exit codes</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2 class="display text-2xl mt-12">From LanguageTool</h2>
  <p class="text-[15px] leading-7 text-[var(--color-text-2)] mt-4">
    Be clear-eyed here: Kotoshu does not compete on grammar. LanguageTool's rule
    library — thousands of grammar, punctuation, and style rules across dozens of
    languages — has no equivalent in Kotoshu today; grammar rule packs are a
    roadmap item, not a feature. What Kotoshu offers is spelling with optional
    semantic reranking of suggestions, resource downloads on demand, and a CI
    story built on exit codes and SARIF. If you need grammar now, run both side
    by side and let each do its half.
  </p>

  <div class="mt-6 rounded-xl border border-[var(--color-border)] bg-[var(--color-bg-inset)] p-5 text-sm leading-7 text-[var(--color-text-2)]">
    <p>
      For the full picture next to all three tools, see
      <a href="/docs/comparison" class="text-[var(--color-accent)] hover:underline">Comparison</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>
